    PowerPC Book I settles the rlwinm upper-half question: it is a DEFECT, not a dispute -- the clearing sentence the 2005 PEM states unconditionally is ABSENT from the manual the PEM itself names as defining
    
    Fetched with the owner's approval on this project's own vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406
    precedent: `_scratchpad/ppc_uisa_book1_v201.pdf' and `.txt' -- PowerPC User Instruction Set
    Architecture, Book I, Version 2.01, September 2003, 997 KB -> 784 KB of text, 12,586 lines.
    VERIFIED LOCALLY rather than taken from any seat's page anchor, which is the rule this
    project applies to every fetched manual.  (`file' reports "10 page(s)" and is wrong, exactly
    as it was for the 21066 manual.)
    
    WHY IT IS AUTHORITATIVE HERE: ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:302 -- "This book does not attempt
    to replace the PowerPC Architecture specification (version 2.01) ... which remains the
    defining manual for the PowerPC Architecture."
    
    BOOK I, :4125-4137, rlwinm:
    
        n   <- SH
        r   <- ROTL32((RS)32:63, n)
        m   <- MASK(MB+32, ME+32)
        RA  <- r & m
    
    and :578 defines MASK as "1s in positions x through y (WRAPPING IF x > y) and 0s
    elsewhere".
    
    *** THE DECIDING FACT IS AN ABSENCE, AND I CHECKED IT THE HARD WAY. ***  Book I NEVER says
    the upper 32 bits of RA are cleared, while the 2005 PEM asserts exactly that,
    unconditionally, in its own instruction description at :30065.  A grep for the obvious
    phrasings supported that negative -- but the rlwinm page span DOES contain several
    "clearing" sentences, and READING them (:4170-4190) shows every one is a programming note
    about clearing "the remaining bits of THE LOW-ORDER 32 BITS of RA", each for an explicitly
    NON-WRAPPING (MB, ME).  That is precisely the false positive a grep-only negative trips on,
    and the instruction to read rather than grep is now in the carrier beside the entry.
    
    CONSEQUENCES:
    
    * `ppcrlwhighhalf' is RESOLVED and RAISED TO HIGH.  It was filed this morning as a dispute
      adopting neither reading; the reading is now settled.  X(rlwinm) holds tmp as uint32_t and
      assigns it into a 64-bit register, which zero-extends, so for MB > ME the upper half reads
      0 where the architecture requires the rotated word.  X(rlwnm) has the same gap.
    * `ppcrlwimiwidth' definitively owes BOTH halves of its repair -- the source narrowing AND
      the high-word insertion.  Narrowing alone is not the instruction's repair.
    * `ppcwitnessmaskoracle': the hold I placed on mask32 this morning is LIFTED, and both
      oracles in #522's committed witness are now corrected.
    
    THE SECOND ORACLE FIX, VERIFIED BY EXHAUSTIVE ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY ARGUMENT:
    mask32 now returns MASK(MB+32, ME+32) at sixty-four bits.  528 of 528 non-wrapping (MB,ME)
    pairs are IDENTICAL to the old body; all 496 wrapping pairs differ; and all 496 agree with
    the old body IN THE LOW WORD.  So the change ADDS the high half and touches nothing else.
    528 + 496 = 1024, the whole space.
    
    AND AN INDEPENDENT CHECK OF THE rldimi REPLACEMENT, run here rather than asked of a seat:
    modelling the proposed do/while literally with a step cap, ALL 4096 (MB,SH) pairs terminate,
    match MASK(MB, 63-SH) exactly, and visit each set position exactly once.  The step cap is
    deliberate -- a loop that will not stop is a RESULT to report, not a hang to suffer.
    
    Honest note on what changed the answer: the design review said it FAVOURED the RTL reading
    and could not establish it from the four local manuals.  It was right on both counts.  What
    settled this was one download and twenty minutes of reading, not a better argument.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
